---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { imageFor } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title="The collectors" description="Milton and Janet Kohn, the Chicago collectors who assembled and exhibited the Famous French Arts collection." image={imageFor(2, 6).md} section="collectors">
  <section id="collectors" style="padding-top:48px">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The collectors</p>
        <h1>Milton &amp; Janet Kohn</h1>
        <p>
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milton_Kohn">Milton Mendel Kohn</a> (1912–2001) was a Chicago architect,
          trained at the Armour Institute of Technology (now the Illinois Institute of Technology). He took up stamp collecting
          in the mid-1960s, on his doctor’s advice to find a relaxing hobby, and the closing line of his title page says the
          same. He is best known as a collector of Holocaust memorabilia, a collection now at the Florida Holocaust
          Museum, where a gallery bears his wife Janet’s name
          (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milton_Kohn">Wikipedia</a>).
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Famous French Arts</em> was a separate project. Milton and Janet Kohn exhibited it internationally in the 1960s and
          1970s. Every page shows his architect’s training: the ruled double borders, the steady capital lettering and the carefully
          mounted proofs. <strong>All of the artwork on the pages was drawn by Milton Kohn himself</strong>, including the
          calligraphic headings, the hand-drawn copies of first-day postmarks, the magnified drawings that explain colour
          varieties, and the colour-pencil sketches of French streets, cathedrals and craftsmen.
        </p>
        <p>
          The exhibit won awards from the start. It received a gold medal at PARFOREX in Park Forest, Illinois, in 1967
          (<a href="https://digital.stamplibrary.org/ContentDmAdmin/ImageProxy.ashx?col=3&ptr=85398&sz=full">American Philatelist, Aug. 1967</a>).
          In 1970 Kohn sent it to <a href="/exhibitions/#philympia">Philympia</a>, the international exhibition in London. It went on to win the Milwaukee Philatelic
          Society President’s Award in 1975
          (<a href="https://digital.stamplibrary.org/ContentDmAdmin/ImageProxy.ashx?col=3&ptr=408548&sz=full">WFSC Bulletin, June 1975</a>),
          a silver at POLPEX ’76 in Chicago, and a bronze at ROCPEX Taipei ’81.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Janet Kohn</strong> was a prize-winning exhibitor in her own right. In November 1978 she won the Grand Award, best
          in show, at SUPEX ’78, the show of the Suburban Collectors Club of Chicago at Brookfield, Illinois, for her exhibit
          “Concentration Camp Censor Markings”
          (<a href="https://digital.stamplibrary.org/ContentDmAdmin/ImageProxy.ashx?col=3&ptr=68959&sz=full">American Philatelist, Jan. 1979, p. 57</a>),
          and in Wisconsin the next month the <em>Wisconsin Federation of Stamp Clubs Bulletin</em> reported “Best in show to
          Janet Kohn” at the University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee Philatelic Society’s show
          (<a href="https://www.wfscstamps.org/ATFP/Bulletin%20subject%20index.pdf">WFSC Bulletin, Winter 1978, p. 1, per the Bulletin subject index</a>).
          She also won medals at international exhibitions in Amsterdam, Prague, Vienna and Taipei.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:8px">
          <a class="btn" href="/exhibitions/">Exhibition record, 1967–1984 →</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="/guide/">How to read an album page →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="mounted" style="margin:0">
        <img src={imageFor(2, 6).md} alt="Album page with the Notre-Dame stamp set inside a Gothic window drawn by Milton Kohn" width="1600" height="1925" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;color:var(--ink-soft);padding:10px 4px 2px">
          Frame 2, page 6: Kohn mounted the Notre-Dame stamp as a pane of his own hand-drawn Gothic window.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

